            <div xml:id="d308">

                <pb facs="0496" n="402" sameAs="frus:frus1924v02/pg402"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.945/1123: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Woods</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1924-05-31T14:00:00+09:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 31, 1924—2
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 31—6:30 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>130. Due to the attitude of the Government as set forth in the last paragraph of
                    my 125, May 28, 4 p.m., there have been no popular anti-American outbreaks but
                    this does not signify that there has been any lessening of deep-seated
                    resentment and bitterness throughout Japan. The nation can be considered to be
                    behind the Government in any protest they may make at Washington and it would
                        <pb facs="0497" n="403" xml:id="pg403"/> be a mistake to interpret their
                    protest as actuated by mainly domestic political urgency.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Woods</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
